<section class="numbered" id="sources">
  <h2><span class="secno">1</span>The sources</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>Four public sources of ready-made images for bioinformatics tools differ mainly in <em>how</em> an image comes
      into existence. The descriptions below are taken from each project's own documentation (numbered references in
      <a href="#references">§7</a>).</p>
    <table class="booktabs sources">
      <caption><span class="figno">Table 1.</span> How images are produced.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>How an image is made</th><th>Base</th><th>Coverage</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">BioContainers</th>
          <td>Automatically, for every Bioconda package build: after <code>conda-build</code> succeeds, the package is added to a
            minimal BusyBox container with <code>mulled-build</code>, the recipe tests are run in that container, and it is
            uploaded to <code>quay.io/biocontainers</code>. Tags combine version and package build
            (<code>1.25--h9dcdb79_0</code>).&nbsp;<sup>[1, 2]</sup></td>
          <td>minimal BusyBox<sup>[2]</sup></td><td>over 8,000 packages<sup>[1]</sup></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Seqera Containers</th>
          <td>On demand, by the Wave service, from a list of Conda or PyPI packages; the default Conda template builds the
            environment with Micromamba and copies it into a minimal base without the package manager.&nbsp;<sup>[3, 4]</sup></td>
          <td>minimal base (template)<sup>[3]</sup></td><td>any Bioconda, conda-forge or PyPI package<sup>[4]</sup></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">StaPH-B</th>
          <td>A hand-written Dockerfile per tool version, contributed by pull request, built and tested in GitHub Actions
            (an <code>app</code> stage and a <code>test</code> stage), pushed to Docker Hub and Quay.&nbsp;<sup>[5, 6]</sup></td>
          <td>usually <code>ubuntu:jammy</code><sup>[6]</sup></td><td>curated by the community</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Capsid</th>
          <td>From a reviewed recipe and a committed lockfile, in public CI: built per architecture, tested, signed with
            Cosign, attested (SPDX SBOM, SLSA provenance), then pulled and tested again on native amd64 and arm64.
            <a href="{{ root }}method/index.html">Method</a></td>
          <td><code>debian:bookworm-slim</code> by digest, apt by snapshot</td><td>{{ inventory.tools }} tools (this hub)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>BioContainers and Seqera Containers are generated from package metadata and therefore cover almost everything
      packaged in Bioconda; Capsid and StaPH-B are curated and cover far less. Capsid's narrower scope is the price of
      reviewing each recipe and publishing, for every image, the evidence described in the next sections.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="terms">
  <h2><span class="secno">2</span>Reproducible, replicable, robust</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <p>The terms are used inconsistently across fields. This page follows <em>The Turing Way</em><sup>[7]</sup>, which
      classifies a result by whether the data and the analysis are the same as in the original work:</p>
    <table class="booktabs matrix">
      <caption><span class="figno">Table 2.</span> Definitions after <em>The Turing Way</em>.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Same data</th><th>Different data</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Same analysis</th><td><strong>Reproducible</strong></td><td><strong>Replicable</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Different analysis</th><td><strong>Robust</strong></td><td><strong>Generalisable</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>A container addresses one ingredient of "the same analysis": the software environment. It cannot make a result
      reproducible on its own (code, parameters, reference data and random seeds matter as much), but without a fixed
      environment neither reproduction nor replication can attribute a difference in results to the data rather than to
      changed software. For a container to serve that purpose, five properties matter:</p>
    <ol class="propositions">
      <li><strong>Identity.</strong> The reference used in an analysis must always resolve to the same bytes. Any OCI
        registry supports this through digests; tags can be moved.</li>
      <li><strong>Description.</strong> What the image contains, package by package, must be knowable without trusting
        the image: otherwise "the same environment" cannot be stated in a methods section.</li>
      <li><strong>Rebuildability.</strong> If the image disappears, it must be possible to rebuild it identically from
        published inputs.</li>
      <li><strong>Provenance.</strong> A user must be able to check that the image was produced by the process that
        claims to have produced it.</li>
      <li><strong>Portability.</strong> The same analysis should run on the hardware available, including ARM servers and
        laptops, with the same software.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="mechanisms">
  <h2><span class="secno">3</span>How Capsid supports each property</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <table class="booktabs">
      <caption><span class="figno">Table 3.</span> Mechanisms in Capsid images, and their limits.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Identity</th><td>Every image page and <code>catalog.json</code> give the digest; tags are only
          moved by a documented republish of the runtime layer.</td><td>Users must reference the digest, not the tag.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Description</th><td>The lockfile (each package by URL and SHA-256) is committed, embedded in
          the image and shown on the image page; an SPDX SBOM is embedded and attested.</td><td>The lockfile covers the tool's
          environment; the Debian runtime is pinned by digest and snapshot and listed only in the SBOM.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Rebuildability</th><td>Recipe, lockfile and engine version determine the Dockerfile; base images
          are pinned by digest, apt by snapshot date, timestamps by <code>SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH</code>.</td><td>Depends on upstream
          artefacts remaining downloadable; source builds can embed build paths.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Provenance</th><td>Keyless Cosign signature bound to the publish workflow, logged in Rekor; SLSA
          build provenance; both verifiable with one command each.</td><td>Proves where and how an image was built, not that
          the upstream sources are sound.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Portability</th><td>linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 under one tag, solved from the same lockfile and
          tested natively on both after publication.</td><td>The same versions on two architectures can still give
          numerically different results (floating-point order, vector instructions); the acceptance test checks that the
          tool works, not bit-identical output.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="measured">
  <h2><span class="secno">4</span>Measured comparison</h2>
  <div class="body">
  {% if comparison %}
    {% set src_label = comparison.sources %}
    <p>The same tool versions were pulled from each source and measured on {{ comparison.measured_at[:10] }} for
      <code>{{ comparison.platform }}</code>: size in the registry and on disk, the platforms offered under the
      reference, the default user, the presence of a Cosign signature or attestation, and the packages and known
      vulnerabilities reported by Trivy {{ (comparison.scanner.version or {}).get("Version", "") }}
      {%- if (comparison.scanner.version or {}).get("VulnerabilityDB") %} (vulnerability database of
      {{ comparison.scanner.version.VulnerabilityDB.UpdatedAt[:10] }}){% endif %}.
      Seqera Containers are not included: their images are created on request by the service, so there is no
      fixed set of published images to measure.</p>

    <table class="booktabs summary">
      <caption><span class="figno">Table 4.</span> Summary per source. Sizes and package counts are medians over the
        images measured. Vulnerabilities are totals of distinct (vulnerability, package) pairs over the images Trivy
        could assess (those with an operating-system package database); the figure after the slash is how many of them
        had a fix available on the measurement date.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Source</th><th class="num">Images</th><th class="num">Compressed</th><th class="num">On disk</th>
          <th class="num">Packages</th><th class="num">arm64</th><th class="num">Non-root</th><th class="num">Signed</th>
          <th class="num">Assessable</th><th class="num">Critical</th><th class="num">High</th><th class="num">Medium</th><th>Base OS</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for s in comparison.summary %}
        <tr{% if s.source == "capsid" %} class="ours"{% endif %}>
          <th scope="row">{{ s.label }}</th><td class="num">{{ s.n }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ s.compressed | bytes }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.uncompressed | bytes }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ s.packages | int }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ pct(s.arm64, s.n) }}</td><td class="num">{{ pct(s.nonroot, s.n) }}</td><td class="num">{{ pct(s.signed, s.n) }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ pct(s.assessed, s.n) }}</td>
          {% if s.assessed %}
          <td class="num">{{ s.vulns.CRITICAL }}<span class="fix">/{{ s.fixable.CRITICAL }}</span></td>
          <td class="num">{{ s.vulns.HIGH }}<span class="fix">/{{ s.fixable.HIGH }}</span></td>
          <td class="num">{{ s.vulns.MEDIUM }}<span class="fix">/{{ s.fixable.MEDIUM }}</span></td>
          {% else %}<td class="num muted" colspan="3">not assessable</td>{% endif %}
          <td class="small">{{ s.os | join("; ") }}{% if s.eosl %} <span class="flag">end of life: {{ s.eosl }}</span>{% endif %}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <table class="booktabs per-tool">
      <caption><span class="figno">Table 5.</span> Per image. Size compressed / on disk; vulnerabilities as
        critical · high · medium, with the number that had a fix available in brackets; ✓ present, — absent.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Source</th><th class="num">Size</th><th>Arch.</th><th>User</th><th>Signed</th>
        <th class="num">Pkgs</th><th class="num">C · H · M</th><th>Base OS</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for r in comparison.results %}
        {% for key in comparison.source_keys if key in r.images %}
        {% set i = r.images[key] %}
        <tr class="{{ 'group-start' if loop.first else '' }}{{ ' ours' if key == 'capsid' else '' }}">
          {% if loop.first %}<th scope="rowgroup" rowspan="{{ r.images | length }}" class="mono">{{ r.tool }}<span class="desc">{{ r.version }}</span></th>{% endif %}
          <td>{{ src_label[key] }}</td>
          {% if i.error is defined %}
          <td colspan="7" class="muted small">not measured: {{ i.error }}</td>
          {% else %}
          <td class="num small">{{ i.compressed_bytes | bytes }} / {{ i.uncompressed_bytes | bytes }}</td>
          <td class="mono small">{{ i.platforms | map("arch") | join(" ") }}</td>
          <td class="mono small">{{ i.user }}</td>
          <td>{{ "✓" if i.cosign_signature else "—" }}</td>
          <td class="num">{{ i.packages }}</td>
          {% set fx = i.vulnerabilities_fixable %}
          <td class="num mono small">{% if i.os_package_db %}{{ i.vulnerabilities.CRITICAL }} · {{ i.vulnerabilities.HIGH }} · {{ i.vulnerabilities.MEDIUM }}
            <span class="fix">({{ fx.get("CRITICAL", 0) + fx.get("HIGH", 0) + fx.get("MEDIUM", 0) }})</span>{% else %}<span class="muted">not assessable</span>{% endif %}</td>
          <td class="small">{{ i.os }}{% if not i.os_package_db %} <span class="muted">(no package database)</span>{% endif %}</td>
          {% endif %}
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Findings</h3>
    <ul class="plain findings">
    {% for s in comparison.summary %}
      <li><strong>{{ s.label }}.</strong> Median {{ s.compressed | bytes }} compressed ({{ s.uncompressed | bytes }} on disk);
        arm64 under the same reference in {{ s.arm64 }} of {{ s.n }} images; non-root default user in {{ s.nonroot }};
        Cosign signature in {{ s.signed }}.
        {% if s.assessed %}Across the {{ s.assessed }} assessable image{{ "" if s.assessed == 1 else "s" }},
        {{ s.vulns.CRITICAL + s.vulns.HIGH }} critical or high findings, of which {{ s.fixable.CRITICAL + s.fixable.HIGH }}
        had a fix available.{% else %}No image could be assessed for system vulnerabilities.{% endif %}</li>
    {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  {% else %}
    <p class="muted">No measurement has been recorded in this hub (<code>benchmarks/results.json</code>).</p>
  {% endif %}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="reading">
  <h2><span class="secno">5</span>Reading the numbers</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>A scanner reports what it can recognise.</strong> Trivy finds operating-system vulnerabilities through
        the base distribution's package database. Most BioContainers images measured here combine a BusyBox userland with a
        Debian <code>/etc/os-release</code> but no <code>dpkg</code> database, so Trivy has no list of system packages to
        check: they are <em>not assessable</em>, not verified clean (Table 4, column <em>Assessable</em>). Tables 4 and 5 therefore count vulnerabilities only for
        images with a package database.</li>
      <li><strong>The tools themselves are hardly covered.</strong> Trivy listed the Conda packages it found (in Capsid
        images, for example, every package of the lockfile) but reported no vulnerability for any Conda package in any
        source. The findings concern the base system, Python packages and statically linked Go binaries, not the compiled
        bioinformatics tools.</li>
      <li><strong>Known is not fixable.</strong> Debian and Ubuntu record many vulnerabilities as <em>affected</em>,
        <em>fix deferred</em> or <em>will not fix</em>; no updated package exists for them. The number with a fix available
        is what rebuilding an image can remove. Counts also depend on the distribution's own severity ratings, which differ
        between Debian and Ubuntu for the same flaw, and change daily with the database date above. They measure exposure,
        not exploitability.</li>
      <li><strong>Size has a cause.</strong> Capsid's runtime is a Debian slim base with <code>bash</code>, <code>tini</code>
        and CA certificates added, chosen so that images behave like a normal Linux system under Nextflow, Snakemake and
        Apptainer. A BusyBox base is smaller; an Ubuntu base with build tools left in place is larger.</li>
      <li><strong>"Signed" means a Cosign signature in the same repository</strong> (<code>sha256-&lt;digest&gt;.sig</code>).
        Other signing schemes would not be detected; none is described in the documentation of the other sources<sup>[1–6]</sup>.</li>
      <li><strong>"Non-root"</strong> is the image's default user. Every image can be run as another user with
        <code>-u</code>; the default matters where a platform refuses root containers.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="numbered" id="choosing">
  <h2><span class="secno">6</span>Choosing a source</h2>
  <div class="body">
    <table class="booktabs">
      <thead><tr><th>If you need</th><th>Prefer</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>a tool that is not in a curated collection</td><td>BioContainers (any Bioconda package) or Seqera Containers (any combination of packages)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>several tools in one image, assembled ad hoc</td><td>Seqera Containers, or a Capsid stack if the tools are in this hub</td></tr>
        <tr><td>public-health pipelines already built on them</td><td>StaPH-B</td></tr>
        <tr><td>a published, checkable record of every package, signature and build provenance, ARM64 under the same tag, a non-root default user</td><td>Capsid</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The sources are not exclusive: a pipeline can take most images from BioContainers and pin the steps whose
      environment must be documented to Capsid images. Missing tools can be <a href="{{ root }}guide/contributing/index.html">proposed</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

